     row of artwork answers it at a glance. The slideshow hid every item
     but one behind a swipe. */
  /* Two shelves, split by type: the row heading carries what a badge
     used to, which frees the caption pill for the thing you actually
     want - the year on a film, the episode on a show. */
  /* Two landscape rows. Fanart, then three lines: when it landed, what
     it is, and what it is about. Falls back to the poster if the feed
     carries no fanart. */
  const UI = window._hemmaUI;
  if (UI) {
    const clean = (t) => String(t || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();

    // default on, so an existing dashboard keeps the marker
    const showWatched = variables.show_watched !== false;
    /* The art carries everything now, so the fields are the two rows
       that sit on it. `aired` is Plex's originallyAvailableAt - the
       ORIGINAL release date - which is a different field from `airdate`,
       the moment it landed in the library. The year comes off the former.
       `rating` arrives pre-formatted as a starred number; under the Plex
       Movie agent it is the Rotten Tomatoes critic score, and it is an
       empty string when Plex has none, which the layout survives. */
    const toCard = (item) => ({
      image: resolveUrl(item.fanart || item.art || item.poster),
      rating: clean(item.rating) || null,
      year: item._isTV ? null
